-- scripts/dst/strict_replay_dst.ail
--
-- WI-A13 stage 3: STRICT REPLAY against `driver_only`. A program discovered
-- from a real driver run is fed back to the real driver through a world
-- reconstituted from it, and the two runs must agree on causal identity and on
-- every recorded projection — under the manifest the program carries.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE RULE THIS SCRIPT EXISTS TO NOT BREAK
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- D2's strict-replay contract is a DETERMINISM contract: "the exact program
-- reproduces the same interaction log, terminal outcome and trace". Determinism
-- is 0-for-18 across eight clusters here, and the reason it fails at THIS stage
-- is structural rather than incidental:
--
--   STRICT REPLAY RE-USES `recording_ports` — THE VERY RECORDER THAT PRODUCED
--   THE PROGRAM. So any gap in the recorder is present on both sides of the
--   comparison, and the comparison passes BECAUSE BOTH SIDES ARE WRONG IN THE
--   SAME WAY.
--
-- Program-versus-log agreement is therefore the recorder grading itself, one
-- level up. This script answers that by grading the REPLAYED RUN with stage 2's
-- independent witnesses — a replayed run is a run, with a ledger trace written
-- by production code, world cursors moved by the response-producing adapters,
-- and a clock — and by carrying a fixture (axis H) in which the replay MATCHES
-- THE PROGRAM EXACTLY AND FAILS THE WITNESS BALANCE. That fixture is the
-- assertion that distinguishes this stage from a tautology; without it, every
-- other check here is satisfied by a recorder and a reconstitution that are
-- wrong together.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE AXES
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
--   A. REFUSAL BEFORE REPLAY. D2: "program decoding and a pure structural
--      validator run before replay". An invalid program, or one whose recorded
--      outcomes cannot be read back, is a REFUSAL at position -1 carrying A9's
--      `InvalidProgram` — never a mismatch at position 0.
--
--   B. THE MANIFEST. Strict replay runs "under the recorded execution
--      manifest/profile", so the program's own manifest is validated against
--      `driver_only()` before the world is built, and A9's `ReplayMetadata` is
--      PROJECTED from it by `replay_metadata_of` rather than restated — so a
--      typed failure cannot carry a profile id that disagrees with the manifest
--      it replayed under.
--
--   C. RECONSTITUTION, BOTH DIRECTIONS. `world_state_of` is the one projection
--      strict replay adds, and `reconstitution_balance` grades it against the
--      program it was built from — under-filled and over-filled from one
--      arithmetic site.
--
--   D. THE REPLAY ITSELF. Identity, origin, kind, request projection and
--      outcome, position by position, plus both ends of the walk: an exhausted
--      program and an unused interaction.
--
--   E. MUTATION (C5). Every replay rule demonstrated red against a deliberate
--      mutation of the REAL replayed log.
--
--   F. NEGATIVE CONTROL (S7). The real, unmutated replay must SURVIVE — and the
--      surviving fixture carries every shape D2 explicitly protects, with no
--      two of its quantities equal. See `rich` below.
--
--   G. WITNESS GRADING OF THE REPLAYED RUN. `check_discovery` over the replay's
--      own log, against witnesses the recorder did not write.
--
--   H. THE TAUTOLOGY CONTROL, and it is this stage's central assertion. A
--      program and a log that share the same defect agree perfectly and fail
--      the witness. Asserted, not claimed.
--
--   I. DETERMINISM, last and least. Two replays of one program agree.
--
--   J. REGRESSION REPLAY (stage 5), D2's SECOND MODE, over the same five real
--      mutated logs axis E just rejected. D2 permits it to "require compatible
--      causal identity, record projection differences, and continue" and binds
--      it with "never weakens tool-call/result correlation or delivers an
--      outcome to a different logical request". So the two modes must disagree
--      on EXACTLY TWO of seven rules and agree on the other five, and grading
--      both over one set of inputs is what makes that legible rather than
--      claimed. The demotion set itself is asserted in `dst_replay`, where
--      demoting a third rule goes red on its own — here the risk is different
--      and worth naming: `replay-unused-interaction` fails SILENTLY without its
--      rule, so a regression mode that dropped it would report a clean, short
--      and entirely fictitious "no differences" on every row of this script.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WITNESSES ON THE Err PATH
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- Stage 2's witnesses for the tool and approval classes are derived from the
-- driver's returned MESSAGE LIST, which is EMPTY when the run ends in `Err`.
-- Stage 2 guarded that by asserting each scenario reached its terminator, which
-- makes the vacuity loud for scenarios that succeed. Stage 3 wants FAILURE-PATH
-- scenarios, so that guard stops being sufficient and is replaced by a general
-- one: every witness a scenario's grading consumes is asserted NON-ZERO where
-- the scenario's control flow reaches that class, on both paths.
--
-- What survives `Err`, verified at HEAD — `TracedSessionResult` is
-- `{ result, trace, world }` and the trace and the world are returned on both
-- paths:
--
--   provider    `ProviderCallPrepared` in the returned ledger trace
--               (session.ail:1998), written by production driver code and
--               emitted separately from the `dispatch_step` call. Strong,
--               cross-component, and unaffected by the outcome.
--   tool        the world's own `tools` cursor delta. Moved by `world_tool`,
--               the response-PRODUCING mechanism, while the log is written by
--               `recording_tool` around it. Medium in cluster 7's sense, and it
--               survives.
--   approval    the tool calls carried by the script entries the run CONSUMED,
--               computed from the initial world's script and the world's own
--               `script` cursor delta. Neither half is the recorder: one is the
--               fixture's input and the other is the provider adapter's cursor.
--               Under a policy that sends every tool call to the approval
--               channel — which every scenario here uses — that is exactly one
--               approval read per tool call.
--   clock       the world's `clock_ms` delta. General across classes.
--
--   `V2ToolDispatchStart` (tool_phase.ail:332) is the strongest tool witness and
--   is emitted BEFORE the port calls, but session.ail passes tool_phase a bare
--   `\event. ledger_emit(session_id, event)` — it goes to the wire and is never
--   appended to the returned trace, so no AILANG assertion can read it. `make
--   strict_replay` consumes it from stdout, which is where that witness lives.

